L = lesson(c1, "earth-rocks", "The earth, rocks and weathering",
    ["Name the layers of the earth", "Describe igneous, sedimentary and metamorphic rocks with examples", "Distinguish weathering from erosion and name the types of weathering"],
    [("text", "The earth has four main layers. The **crust** is the thin, solid outer skin on which we live. Under it lies the **mantle**, a thick layer of very hot rock that can flow very slowly. Below the mantle is the **outer core**, which is liquid, and at the centre the **inner core**, which is solid."),
     ("key", "definition", "Three families of rock", "- **Igneous rocks** form when molten rock (magma or lava) cools and hardens. Granite cooled slowly underground; basalt cooled quickly at the surface.\n- **Sedimentary rocks** form from layers of sand, mud or shells pressed together over time. Examples: sandstone, shale, limestone.\n- **Metamorphic rocks** are old rocks changed by great heat and pressure. Examples: marble (from limestone) and slate (from shale)."),
     ("text", "**Weathering** is the breaking down of rock **in place**, by the weather, plants and chemicals. **Erosion** is the wearing away and carrying of material by rivers, waves, wind or ice. In weathering nothing is transported; in erosion there is movement."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Types of weathering", "- **Physical (mechanical):** rocks crack when heated by day and cooled at night; outer layers may peel off like the skin of an onion (exfoliation).\n- **Chemical:** rainwater is slightly acid and dissolves some rocks, especially limestone (carbonation). In hot, wet regions chemical weathering is very fast and produces deep red soils.\n- **Biological:** roots grow into cracks and widen them."),
     ("key", "pieges", "Common mistakes", "- Weathering is not erosion: weathering happens without transport.\n- Basalt and granite are both igneous; do not call granite 'metamorphic'.\n- Chemical weathering is fastest where it is both hot and wet, such as the equatorial region of southern Cameroon.")],
    [("Example 1: classify rocks", "A student finds a rock made of layers of sand grains in a river bank near Bamenda. Which family does it belong to and why?",
      ["Layers of sand grains pressed together point to deposition of sediments.", "Rocks made of compacted sediments are sedimentary.", "The probable rock is sandstone."],
      "Sedimentary rock (probably sandstone)."),
     ("Example 2: identify the weathering", "On a hot day a rock face in the Far North reaches a very high temperature; at night it cools quickly and flakes appear. Name the process.",
      ["Large daily temperature changes make the outer rock expand and contract.", "No chemicals or plants are involved, so it is physical weathering.", "Flaking in layers is called exfoliation."],
      "Physical weathering (exfoliation).")],
    (fig, "Layers of the earth (not to scale)", "Concentric circles showing crust, mantle, outer core and inner core."),
    [MCQ("Which layer of the earth is liquid?", "The outer core", ["The crust", "The mantle", "The inner core"], "The outer core is liquid; the inner core is solid because of the pressure."),
     TF("Limestone is an igneous rock.", False, "Limestone is sedimentary, formed from shells and calcium-rich sediments."),
     MATCH("Match each rock to its family.", [("Granite", "Igneous"), ("Sandstone", "Sedimentary"), ("Marble", "Metamorphic")], "Granite cooled from magma, sandstone is compacted sand, marble is limestone changed by heat and pressure."),
     MCQ("Rainwater slowly dissolves limestone. This is an example of", "Chemical weathering", ["Physical weathering", "Erosion by waves", "Deposition"], "Dissolving by weak acid in rainwater is chemical weathering (carbonation)."),
     OPEN("Explain the difference between weathering and erosion.", "Weathering is the breaking down of rock where it lies, caused by temperature change, water, chemicals or living things. Erosion is the removal and transport of rock material by agents such as rivers, waves, wind or ice. Weathering supplies the loose material that erosion carries away.", "2 marks for defining weathering (in place, no transport); 2 marks for erosion (transport by an agent); 1 mark for a link or example.", points=5),
     PROB("Study these three rock samples: A formed when lava cooled quickly on the surface; B is made of compacted layers of mud; C was once limestone but was heated deep underground.",
          [("mcq", "Sample A is most likely", "basalt", ["limestone", "slate", "shale"], "Lava that cools quickly gives a fine-grained igneous rock such as basalt.", {}),
           ("mcq", "Sample B belongs to the family of", "sedimentary rocks", ["igneous rocks", "metamorphic rocks", "volcanic rocks"], "Compacted mud forms sedimentary rock (shale).", {}),
           ("tf", "Sample C is a metamorphic rock.", True, "Limestone changed by heat and pressure becomes marble, a metamorphic rock.", {}),
           ("open", "Give one way in which sample B could be broken down by weathering.", "Rain could seep into cracks and chemicals could slowly dissolve minerals, or plant roots could widen cracks until pieces break off.", "1 mark for a valid process; 1 mark for a short explanation.", {})])],
    [("Which is the thinnest layer of the earth?", "The crust", ["The mantle", "The outer core", "The inner core"], "The crust is very thin compared with the other layers."),
     ("Which rock is metamorphic?", "Slate", ["Basalt", "Granite", "Sandstone"], "Slate forms from shale under heat and pressure."),
     ("Peeling of rock surfaces because of temperature changes is called", "Exfoliation", ["Carbonation", "Deposition", "Transportation"], "Exfoliation is a form of physical weathering."),
     ("Roots widening a crack in a rock is", "Biological weathering", ["Chemical weathering", "Erosion", "Deposition"], "Living things such as plants cause biological weathering."),
     ("Chemical weathering is fastest in", "Hot and wet regions", ["Cold and dry regions", "Hot and dry regions only", "Polar regions"], "Heat and water speed up chemical reactions.")],
    ["Cameroon examples of rock types (e.g. volcanic basalt of the western highlands) are only mentioned in general terms; a teacher should check local examples against the syllabus."])

