L = lesson(c1, "rivers", "Rivers: course and landforms",
    ["Define source, tributary, confluence, mouth and watershed", "Describe the work of a river in its upper, middle and lower courses", "Name the main river landforms and explain how a meander and an ox-bow lake form"],
    [("text", "A river begins at its **source**, often in highlands. **Tributaries** join it and the meeting point is a **confluence**. The river ends at its **mouth**, where it enters a sea or lake. The high ground that separates two river basins is the **watershed**. Water drains from a watershed into the **river basin**."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Upper, middle and lower course", "- **Upper course:** steep, fast, mainly vertical erosion. Features: V-shaped valleys, rapids, waterfalls, interlocking spurs.\n- **Middle course:** gentler slope; the river swings from side to side and forms **meanders**.\n- **Lower course:** flat land; much deposition. Features: wide floodplain, levees, ox-bow lakes, delta or estuary."),
     ("text", "**Waterfalls** form where a layer of hard rock lies over softer rock: the soft rock is worn away faster and the water plunges over the edge. A **meander** is a bend: erosion is strongest on the outside, deposition on the inside. When a loop is cut off at a narrow neck, it becomes an **ox-bow lake**."),
     ("key", "definition", "Estuary and delta", "- An **estuary** is the wide, tidal mouth of a river where fresh water meets the sea; the Wouri estuary near Douala is an example.\n- A **delta** is a fan of deposited sediment built at the mouth when the river drops more material than the sea removes."),
     ("key", "attention", "Be careful", "- Do not say that a river erodes only in the upper course: it erodes in every course, but the type and place of erosion change.\n- A tributary joins the main river; a distributary leaves it (as in a delta).\n- The Benue flows north to join the Niger in Nigeria, but the Sanaga and the Wouri flow to the Atlantic.")],
    [("Example 1: name the feature", "Water falls over a hard layer of rock and a deep plunge pool lies below. In which course is this feature most likely, and how did it form?",
      ["A waterfall needs a steep gradient, which is typical of the upper course.", "Hard rock lies over softer rock; the soft rock is eroded faster.", "The overhang collapses and the waterfall retreats upstream."],
      "A waterfall in the upper course, formed by differential erosion."),
     ("Example 2: sequence of an ox-bow lake", "Put in order: A) the neck is cut by a flood, B) the meander grows larger, C) the loop is sealed by deposition and becomes a lake, D) erosion on the outside bend and deposition inside.",
      ["The process starts with erosion and deposition on a bend (D).", "The meander then grows larger and the neck narrows (B).", "A flood cuts through the neck (A) and the cut-off loop is sealed (C)."],
      "D, B, A, C.")],
    (fig, "Long profile of a river (schematic)", "A sloping line shows the river falling from its source through three courses to its mouth."),
    [MCQ("The meeting point of two rivers is called a", "Confluence", ["Source", "Watershed", "Delta"], "A confluence is where a tributary joins the main river."),
     TF("Meanders are mainly found in the upper course.", False, "Meanders are typical of the middle and lower courses."),
     MATCH("Match each feature to its course.", [("V-shaped valley", "Upper course"), ("Meander", "Middle course"), ("Floodplain", "Lower course")], "Steep valleys are in the upper course, bends in the middle, and wide flat land in the lower course."),
     MCQ("An ox-bow lake is formed from", "A cut-off meander", ["A waterfall plunge pool", "A glacier", "A volcanic crater"], "A meander loop abandoned after the neck is cut becomes an ox-bow lake."),
     OPEN("Describe how a delta is formed.", "When a river enters a sea or lake it slows down and loses energy. It drops (deposits) the sediment it carries. If the sea does not remove this material, it builds up into a fan-shaped area of new land and the river splits into several channels (distributaries).", "2 marks for the slowing river and deposition; 2 marks for build-up and distributaries; 1 mark for the condition (sea not removing the sediment).", points=5),
     PROB("A river rises on a high plateau, flows over a hard rock band, crosses a wide flat plain and enters the sea.",
          [("mcq", "A waterfall would most probably form", "where the river crosses the hard rock band", ["on the wide flat plain", "at the mouth", "in the sea"], "Waterfalls form where hard rock overlies softer rock.", {}),
           ("tf", "Deposition is greater than erosion on the wide flat plain.", True, "On flat land the river is slow and drops sediment, building a floodplain.", {}),
           ("mcq", "The wide, tidal mouth where the river meets the sea is", "an estuary", ["a watershed", "a tributary", "a spur"], "An estuary is a funnel-shaped mouth affected by tides.", {}),
           ("open", "Give two ways in which the river is useful to people living on the plain.", "Water for drinking and farming, fertile alluvial soil for crops, fishing, transport, and possibly hydroelectric power upstream.", "1 mark each for two valid uses.", {})])],
    [("The high ground separating two river basins is the", "Watershed", ["Confluence", "Levee", "Delta"], "The watershed marks the limit of a river basin."),
     ("Which feature is found in the upper course?", "Rapids and waterfalls", ["Ox-bow lakes", "Deltas", "Wide floodplains"], "Steep gradients produce rapids and waterfalls."),
     ("Erosion is greatest on the outside of a meander because", "The water flows faster there", ["The water is slower there", "The land is flat", "The river is frozen"], "Fast water on the outside bank undercuts it."),
     ("Which river in Cameroon flows into the Niger system in Nigeria?", "The Benue", ["The Sanaga", "The Wouri", "The Nyong"], "The Benue flows west to meet the Niger; the others flow to the Atlantic."),
     ("Natural raised banks beside the river in the lower course are", "Levees", ["Spurs", "Gorges", "Watersheds"], "Levees build up when floods deposit coarse material beside the channel.")],
    ["Names of Cameroonian rivers and their destination (Benue to the Niger; Sanaga, Wouri, Nyong to the Atlantic) to be verified on a map by a teacher."])

