L = lesson(c1, "coasts", "Coasts: erosion and deposition",
    ["Describe how waves erode and deposit", "Explain how cliffs, caves, arches and stacks form", "Explain longshore drift and the formation of beaches and spits"],
    [("text", "Waves are caused by wind blowing over the sea. **Destructive waves** are steep and strong, and they erode the coast. **Constructive waves** are low and gentle; they push material up the beach and build it. Waves erode by **hydraulic action** (force of water and trapped air), **abrasion** (hurling stones), **attrition** (stones wearing each other) and **solution** (dissolving)."),
     ("key", "retenir", "From cliff to stack", "1. Waves attack the base of a cliff and cut a notch.\n2. The notch grows into a cave.\n3. A cave cut through a headland becomes an arch.\n4. The roof of the arch collapses, leaving a stack.\n5. The stack is worn down to a stump.\nThe cliff retreats and leaves a flat **wave-cut platform**."),
     ("text", "Deposition builds beaches of sand and pebbles. Waves often hit the shore at an angle: the swash carries material up the beach at that angle, and the backwash pulls it straight down. Little by little the material moves along the coast. This is **longshore drift**."),
     ("key", "definition", "Spit", "A **spit** is a long ridge of sand or shingle joined to the land at one end and stretching into the sea or across a river mouth. It forms where the coast changes direction and longshore drift carries material beyond the bend."),
     ("key", "attention", "Be careful", "- Erosion forms cliffs, caves, arches and stacks; deposition forms beaches and spits. Do not mix them up.\n- Mangrove swamps along the Cameroon coast protect the shore and are a habitat for fish; they are sheltered, muddy coasts rather than rocky cliffs.\n- Beaches near Limbe have dark sand because it comes from volcanic rock.")],
    [("Example 1: sequence", "Arrange the stages: stack, arch, cave, wave-cut notch.", ["Waves first cut a notch at the base.", "The notch becomes a cave, then a cave cut through a headland is an arch.", "The arch roof collapses leaving a stack."], "Notch, cave, arch, stack."),
     ("Example 2: longshore drift", "Waves arrive from the south-west at an angle to a beach running north-south. In which direction is material moved overall?", ["Swash carries material up the beach in the direction of the waves, towards the north-east.", "Backwash pulls it back at right angles, so it is carried a little further along each time.", "The overall movement follows the angle of the incoming waves along the coast."], "Material moves along the beach in the general direction the waves are travelling (northwards).")],
    (fig, "A cliff and wave-cut platform", "A cliff with a wave-cut platform at its base on a sea coast."),
    [MCQ("Which wave type builds beaches?", "Constructive waves", ["Destructive waves", "Tidal waves only", "Waves never build beaches"], "Constructive waves have strong swash and weak backwash, so material is added."),
     TF("A stack is formed after the roof of an arch collapses.", True, "The remaining pillar of rock is a stack."),
     MATCH("Match each process to its meaning.", [("Abrasion", "Stones thrown against the cliff"), ("Attrition", "Stones wearing each other down"), ("Hydraulic action", "Force of water in cracks")], "Each process has a different mechanism of erosion."),
     MCQ("Movement of material along a beach by waves arriving at an angle is", "Longshore drift", ["Weathering", "Mass movement", "Attrition"], "This is the definition of longshore drift."),
     OPEN("Explain how a spit is formed.", "Longshore drift carries sand and shingle along the coast. Where the coastline bends or a river mouth interrupts it, the material continues straight on into deeper water and is deposited, building a ridge joined to land at one end. Waves can curve its end into a hook.", "2 marks for longshore drift; 2 marks for deposition at a change in direction; 1 mark for the shape (attached at one end).", points=5),
     PROB("Waves are cutting into a rocky headland. A cave has formed, and on a nearby part of the coast there is a wide sandy beach.",
          [("mcq", "The next stage after the cave breaks through the headland is", "an arch", ["a spit", "a delta", "a levee"], "A cave cut right through becomes an arch.", {}),
           ("tf", "The wave-cut platform is formed by deposition.", False, "It is a flat rock surface left by cliff retreat, so erosion forms it.", {}),
           ("mcq", "The sandy beach is most likely built by", "constructive waves", ["destructive waves", "glaciers", "rivers in the upper course"], "Gentle waves add material.", {}),
           ("open", "Give one reason why mangroves are important on the Cameroon coast.", "They protect the shore from waves and storms, trap sediment, and shelter fish and other wildlife.", "1 mark for any valid reason; 1 mark for a brief explanation.", {})])],
    [("A wave-cut platform is formed by", "Erosion", ["Deposition", "Volcanic activity", "Folding"], "Cliff retreat leaves a platform."),
     ("Which comes first in the sequence?", "Notch at the cliff base", ["Stack", "Stump", "Arch"], "The sequence starts with a notch."),
     ("Hydraulic action is erosion by", "The force of water and trapped air", ["Dissolving", "Stones hitting rock", "Wind blowing sand"], "Hydraulic action works on cracks."),
     ("A spit is", "A ridge of sand joined to land at one end", ["A rocky pillar", "A cave", "A tall cliff"], "Spits are depositional features."),
     ("Dark sand on beaches near Limbe is because", "It comes from volcanic rock", ["It is polluted", "It is coal", "It is sand from the Sahara"], "Volcanic rock fragments are dark.")],
    ["Teacher to verify the Limbe black-sand statement and whether local coastal case studies are required by the syllabus."])

