L = lesson(c1, "volcanoes-earthquakes", "Volcanoes, earthquakes and plate movements",
    ["Describe the earth's plates and the three types of plate boundary", "Describe the parts of a volcano and the main types of eruption product", "Explain the causes and effects of earthquakes", "Link these ideas to Mount Cameroon and the Cameroon Volcanic Line"],
    [("text", "The crust is broken into large pieces called **plates**. They move very slowly (a few centimetres a year) because of heat currents in the mantle. Most volcanoes, earthquakes and fold mountains occur near the plate boundaries."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Plate boundaries", "- **Constructive (divergent):** plates move apart; magma rises and makes new crust (mid-ocean ridges, rift valleys).\n- **Destructive (convergent):** plates move together; one may sink under the other, producing volcanoes, earthquakes, ocean trenches or fold mountains.\n- **Conservative (transform):** plates slide past each other; earthquakes are common, volcanoes are not."),
     ("text", "A **volcano** is an opening where magma reaches the surface. Magma rises through a **vent** to the **crater**. Eruptions throw out lava, ash, gas and volcanic bombs. A volcano is **active** if it has erupted in recent history, **dormant** if it is quiet but may erupt again, and **extinct** if it is not expected to erupt."),
     ("text", "An **earthquake** is a shaking of the ground caused by a sudden release of energy as rocks move along a break called a **fault**. The point underground where it starts is the **focus**; the point directly above it on the surface is the **epicentre**. Earthquakes are recorded by a **seismograph** and their strength is given as a magnitude."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Cameroon", "Mount Cameroon, near Buea, is an active volcano and the highest mountain in West and Central Africa; it has erupted several times in recent decades. It belongs to the **Cameroon Volcanic Line** of mountains and crater lakes that runs from the coast towards the north-east. Lake Nyos is a crater lake where a deadly release of gas occurred in 1986."),
     ("key", "pieges", "Common mistakes", "- Do not confuse the focus (underground) with the epicentre (on the surface).\n- Volcanoes are not found only at plate boundaries (there are hotspots), but most are.\n- Fold mountains form where plates push together, not where they move apart.")],
    [("Example 1: identify the boundary", "Two plates are moving towards each other and one is being pushed under the other. A line of volcanoes and a deep ocean trench lie along the boundary. Which type of boundary is this?", ["The plates move towards each other, so it is not constructive or conservative.", "One plate sinks (subduction) and melts, feeding volcanoes.", "Therefore it is a destructive boundary."], "A destructive (convergent) plate boundary."),
     ("Example 2: focus and epicentre", "An earthquake begins 10 km below the ground. A town stands directly above that point. Which word describes the town's location, and which word describes the point 10 km below?", ["The point below the ground where the earthquake starts is the focus.", "The point on the surface directly above the focus is the epicentre.", "The town is at the epicentre, so it will probably feel the strongest shaking."], "Epicentre (town); focus (10 km below).")],
    (fig, "Cross-section of a volcano (schematic)", "A cone-shaped volcano with a vent, crater, lava flow and magma chamber."),
    [MCQ("At which type of plate boundary do plates move apart?", "Constructive", ["Destructive", "Conservative", "There is no such boundary"], "Divergent or constructive boundaries make new crust."),
     TF("The epicentre is the point where an earthquake begins underground.", False, "That point is the focus; the epicentre is on the surface above it."),
     MATCH("Match each term to its meaning.", [("Crater", "Bowl-shaped opening at the top of a volcano"), ("Seismograph", "Instrument that records earthquakes"), ("Fault", "Crack in the crust where rocks move")], "These are basic volcano and earthquake terms."),
     MCQ("Which of these is a mountain in Cameroon that is an active volcano?", "Mount Cameroon", ["Mount Kilimanjaro", "Mount Oku", "Mount Manengouba"], "Mount Cameroon is the known active volcano; Oku and Manengouba are volcanic but not recently active, and Kilimanjaro is in Tanzania."),
     OPEN("Describe two effects of a major volcanic eruption on people.", "Lava and ash can destroy homes, farms and roads, and people may have to leave the area. Ash can harm health and aircraft. In the long term volcanic ash makes soils fertile and the mountain can attract tourists.", "1 mark for each valid negative effect (two) and 1 mark for a benefit.", points=3),
     PROB("A fold mountain range lies along a boundary where two continental plates collide. Earthquakes are frequent there.",
          [("mcq", "The boundary is", "destructive", ["constructive", "conservative", "passive"], "Collision is a convergent (destructive) boundary.", {}),
           ("tf", "Fold mountains are formed when layers of rock are squeezed and pushed up by the collision.", True, "Compression crumples the rock layers into folds.", {}),
           ("mcq", "Earthquakes are recorded with a", "seismograph", ["barometer", "rain gauge", "hygrometer"], "A seismograph measures ground shaking.", {}),
           ("open", "Give two ways to reduce the damage from earthquakes.", "Build houses and bridges with earthquake-resistant designs, plan land use away from faults, educate people about safety drills, and set up warning and emergency plans.", "1 mark each for two sensible measures.", {})])],
    [("The thin outer layer of the earth is the", "Crust", ["Mantle", "Inner core", "Outer core"], "The crust is the outermost layer."),
     ("A volcano which has not erupted for a very long time and is not expected to erupt is", "Extinct", ["Active", "Dormant", "Constructive"], "Extinct volcanoes are finished."),
     ("What is magma called when it reaches the surface?", "Lava", ["Ash", "Shale", "Granite"], "Magma on the surface is lava."),
     ("Sliding plates (transform boundary) mainly cause", "Earthquakes", ["Volcanoes", "New oceans", "Rivers"], "Friction causes earthquakes along transform faults."),
     ("Lake Nyos is in Cameroon and is a", "Crater lake", ["Man-made lake", "Coastal lagoon", "Lake in the Sahel"], "It fills a volcanic crater.")],
    ["Mount Cameroon height (about 4,000 m), its location near Buea, 'highest in West and Central Africa' and the 1986 Nyos event are to be verified. The explanation of the Cameroon Volcanic Line is deliberately general."])

