L = lesson(c2, "climates-vegetation", "Climate and vegetation zones of the tropics",
    ["Describe equatorial and tropical continental (savanna) climates", "Describe the vegetation of the equatorial rainforest and the savanna", "Explain how climate controls vegetation and how people use these zones"],
    [("text", "Near the equator the **equatorial climate** is hot, wet and humid all year. The temperature is high (around the mid-twenties °C) and varies little between months; the yearly range is small. Rainfall is heavy, well over 1,500 mm a year, and often convectional. In southern Cameroon there are two rainy seasons and two drier seasons."),
     ("text", "Farther from the equator the **tropical continental (savanna) climate** has a clear wet season and dry season. The dry season is longer towards the north. The hot dry **harmattan** wind blows from the Sahara in the dry season, bringing dust and cooler nights; in the wet season moist winds blow from the south-west Atlantic."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Natural vegetation", "- **Tropical rainforest:** tall, evergreen, broad-leaved hardwood trees (e.g. mahogany, iroko, sapele) in several layers; dense, dark forest floor; rich in species.\n- **Savanna:** tall grasses with scattered trees such as baobab and acacia; the trees have thick bark, long roots and small leaves to resist drought and fire.\n- **Sahel (semi-desert):** short grass, thorny shrubs and few trees; erratic rainfall."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Using the vegetation", "Forests give timber, food, medicines and homes for wildlife but suffer from logging and clearing for farms. Savanna supports cattle, cotton and grain crops and is home to wildlife in parks, but is damaged by overgrazing, bush fires and drought. Both need careful management."),
     ("key", "pieges", "Common mistakes", "- 'Equatorial' does not mean 'desert-hot': it means hot and wet.\n- Savanna trees are not evergreen rainforest trees; many lose leaves in the dry season.\n- Do not say that the rainforest has no seasons: the temperature is similar all year, but the amount of rain still varies.")],
    [("Example 1: explain a savanna tree", "Explain two features of an acacia tree that help it survive in the savanna.", ["Savanna has a long dry season, so trees need ways to save water.", "Small leaves or leaf-fall in the dry season reduce water loss.", "Long roots reach deep water and thick bark protects against grass fires."], "Small leaves/leaf-fall and long roots/thick bark."),
     ("Example 2: climate and vegetation link", "Why does the equatorial region have tall dense forest while the northern part of Cameroon has grassland?", ["The south has heavy rain nearly all year, so trees grow tall and green.", "The north has a long dry season and less rain.", "Grass recovers quickly after drought and fire, but dense forest cannot be supported."], "More rain and a short dry season in the south support forest; less rain and a long dry season in the north support savanna.")],
    (fig, "Layers of the tropical rainforest", "Stacked bands for emergent trees, canopy, understorey, shrub layer and forest floor."),
    [MCQ("Which climate has heavy rain all year round and a very small temperature range?", "Equatorial climate", ["Savanna climate", "Desert climate", "Mountain climate with snow"], "These are the main features of the equatorial climate."),
     TF("The harmattan is a hot, dry, dusty wind from the Sahara.", True, "It blows in the dry season over West and Central Africa."),
     MATCH("Match each zone to a typical plant.", [("Rainforest", "Mahogany"), ("Savanna", "Baobab"), ("Sahel", "Thorny shrubs")], "Each zone has typical vegetation."),
     MCQ("The highest layer of the rainforest is the", "Emergent layer", ["Shrub layer", "Forest floor", "Understorey"], "Emergent trees stick out above the canopy."),
     OPEN("Explain two ways in which people use the savanna.", "People graze cattle and other animals on the grass, and grow crops such as cotton, millet and maize in the wet season. The savanna also supports wildlife tourism in national parks.", "2 marks for each valid use with a reason; 1 mark for a Cameroon example.", points=5),
     PROB("Two stations: Station X has about 2,500 mm of rain a year and a temperature range of 2 °C. Station Y has about 900 mm in a single wet season of five months and a larger range.",
          [("mcq", "Station X most probably has", "equatorial climate", ["savanna climate", "Sahel climate", "polar climate"], "Heavy rain all year and a small range indicate equatorial climate.", {}),
           ("mcq", "The natural vegetation near Station Y is probably", "savanna", ["rainforest", "mangrove forest", "ice cap"], "A single wet season and a long dry season produce savanna.", {}),
           ("tf", "Station Y has a longer dry season than Station X.", True, "Y has only a five-month wet season.", {}),
           ("open", "Suggest one problem for farmers at Station Y.", "Droughts and unreliable rainfall can damage crops, and soil can erode in the dry season when vegetation is thin.", "1 mark for the problem; 1 mark for the reason.", {})])],
    [("Tropical rainforest trees are mostly", "Evergreen hardwoods", ["Thorny shrubs", "Grasses only", "Conifers"], "They keep their leaves and have hard wood."),
     ("The savanna has", "A wet season and a dry season", ["Rain every day", "Snow in winter", "No rain at all"], "This marks the tropical continental climate."),
     ("A baobab is typical of", "Savanna", ["Mangrove swamps", "Equatorial forest floor", "Mountain peaks"], "The baobab survives drought by storing water."),
     ("Forests are cleared in tropical Africa mainly for", "Farmland and timber", ["Skiing", "Making deserts", "Building glaciers"], "Logging and farming are the main causes."),
     ("The Sahel is the zone", "Between the savanna and the Sahara", ["On the Atlantic coast", "At the equator", "On Mount Cameroon"], "It is a semi-arid belt south of the Sahara.")],
    ["Rainfall thresholds (e.g. 'above about 1,500 mm') and the number of rainy seasons per Cameroonian region are simplified; a teacher should check them against the syllabus. Tree names are typical examples."])

