L = lesson(c3, "contours-sections", "Contours, relief and cross-sections",
    ["Explain what contour lines show", "Recognise steep and gentle slopes, valleys, spurs and peaks from contours", "Calculate gradient", "Draw and interpret a cross-section and check intervisibility"],
    [("text", "A **contour line** joins places that have the same height above sea level. The **contour interval** is the vertical height between two lines (for example 20 m). Heights on a map are measured from sea level. A **spot height** gives the exact height of a point and a **trig point** is a surveyed marker on a summit."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Reading the shape of the land", "- Contours **close together**: steep slope. **Far apart**: gentle slope.\n- Closed rings with higher numbers inside: hill or peak.\n- **V-shapes pointing uphill** mark a valley (a river runs in it).\n- Contours bulging downhill mark a **spur**.\n- Evenly spaced lines: uniform slope.\n- A sudden merging of lines: cliff or escarpment."),
     ("key", "formule", "Gradient", "Gradient = vertical interval ÷ horizontal distance, with both in the same unit. A rise of 100 m over 3 000 m gives 100 ÷ 3 000, or 1 in 30.", "\\text{gradient}=\\frac{\\text{vertical interval}}{\\text{horizontal distance}}"),
     ("text", "A **cross-section** is a side view along a line drawn between two points. To draw it: lay a strip of paper along the line, mark each contour crossing with its height, transfer the marks to graph paper, plot the heights, and join the points smoothly. Use a suitable vertical scale and label the axes. Two places are **intervisible** if no higher ground lies in between on the section."),
     ("key", "attention", "Be careful", "- Contour lines never cross (except at a cliff).\n- Do not join the ends of the profile with straight lines; the real land is curved.\n- Vertical exaggeration makes slopes look steeper on the section than they are; state the vertical scale.")],
    [("Example 1: calculate gradient", "Point A is at 100 m and point B is at 400 m. The horizontal distance between them is 3 km. Find the gradient as 1 in n.", ["Vertical interval = 400 − 100 = 300 m.", "Horizontal distance = 3 km = 3 000 m.", "Gradient = 300 : 3 000 = 1 : 10, so 1 in 10."], "1 in 10."),
     ("Example 2: steep or gentle?", "Along the line PQ the 100 m and 200 m contours are 1 cm apart; along the line RS they are 4 cm apart on the same map. Which line is steeper?", ["The same height change happens over a shorter distance on PQ.", "A shorter horizontal distance for the same rise means a steeper slope.", "So PQ is steeper than RS."], "PQ is steeper.")],
    (fig, "Contour lines on a hill (schematic)", "Concentric rings that are close together on the left side and wide apart on the right."),
    [MCQ("Contour lines that are very close together show", "A steep slope", ["A gentle slope", "A flat plain", "The sea"], "Many height changes in a short distance mean steepness."),
     TF("Contour lines join places of the same height above sea level.", True, "That is the definition of a contour."),
     NUM("Point A is at 250 m and point B at 700 m. What is the height difference in metres?", 450, "700 − 250 = 450 m.", unit="m"),
     NUM("The vertical interval is 200 m and the horizontal distance is 4 km. Give the gradient as 1 in n (enter n).", 20, "4 km = 4 000 m; 4 000 ÷ 200 = 20, so the gradient is 1 in 20.", tier="approfondissement"),
     MCQ("V-shaped contours pointing uphill indicate", "A valley", ["A spur", "A summit", "A lake"], "Contours bend upstream (uphill) across a valley.", tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("Along a section line the heights in order are: 100 m (start), 200 m, 300 m (a ridge), 150 m, 100 m (end).",
          [("mcq", "The highest point on the line is at", "300 m", ["100 m", "150 m", "200 m"], "The ridge is at 300 m.", {}),
           ("num", "The total rise from start to the ridge is how many metres?", 200, "300 − 100 = 200 m.", 0, "m", {}),
           ("tf", "A 150 m hill beyond the ridge can be seen from the start point if the ridge is higher.", False, "The 300 m ridge blocks the view; they are not intervisible.", {}),
           ("open", "State two steps when drawing a cross-section.", "Lay paper along the line and mark contour crossings with heights; transfer them to graph paper, plot the points and join with a smooth line, labelling the scales.", "1 mark each for two correct steps.", {})])],
    [("The contour interval is", "The height difference between two contours", ["The scale of the map", "The map's north line", "The length of a river"], "It is the vertical spacing between adjacent lines."),
     ("A gentle slope has contours which are", "Far apart", ["Close together", "Crossing each other", "Straight and parallel at 90°"], "Wide spacing means a gentle slope."),
     ("A rise of 50 m over 1 000 m is a gradient of", "1 in 20", ["1 in 50", "1 in 5", "1 in 200"], "1 000 ÷ 50 = 20."),
     ("Two points are intervisible if", "No higher ground lies between them", ["They are on the same map", "They are the same height", "A river runs between them"], "Look for blocking high ground on the section."),
     ("A spot height on a map shows", "The height of a point", ["The slope", "The distance", "The river speed"], "It is a marked exact height.")],
    ["Teacher to confirm the exam's conventions for gradient and section drawing (ratio or fraction, vertical scale)."])

