L = lesson(c4, "agriculture", "Agriculture in Cameroon and West/Central Africa",
    ["Distinguish subsistence, commercial and plantation farming", "Name the main food and cash crops and where they grow", "Describe farming methods and the problems of farmers", "Suggest ways of improving agriculture"],
    [("text", "Agriculture is the main activity of many Cameroonians. **Subsistence farming** produces food mainly for the farmer's family (cassava, plantain, maize, yams, groundnuts, beans). **Commercial farming** produces crops for sale (cocoa, coffee, cotton). **Plantation farming** uses large estates, many workers and often processing on site (banana, oil palm, rubber, tea)."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Crops and regions", "- Cocoa: forest zone of the Centre, South and Southwest.\n- Coffee: Robusta in forest and coastal zones; Arabica in the western highlands.\n- Cotton: savanna of the North and Far North.\n- Banana, oil palm, rubber and tea: plantations around Mount Cameroon, Mungo and other humid, volcanic areas.\n- Cattle and other livestock: Adamawa and North West grasslands.\n- Fishing: the Atlantic coast and rivers and lakes."),
     ("text", "Traditional methods include **shifting cultivation** (clearing a plot, farming it for a few years, then moving on), **bush fallowing**, **mixed cropping** and use of hoe and cutlass. Modern methods include fertilisers, improved seeds, irrigation, tractors and crop rotation. Mixed farming combines crops and livestock on one farm."),
     ("key", "pieges", "Problems of farmers", "- Poor roads, so crops rot before reaching the market.\n- Pests and diseases; soil exhaustion.\n- Falling or unstable prices of cash crops.\n- Little credit and few modern inputs; land shortage in some areas.\n- Climate risks: droughts in the north, floods in low areas.\nDo not blame the weather alone in an exam answer: give human and economic causes as well."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Improving agriculture", "Better roads and storage, training and extension workers, farmers' cooperatives, credit, improved seeds and fertilisers, processing crops locally to add value, irrigation in dry areas, and diversifying crops so that income does not depend on one product.")],
    [("Example 1: classify", "Classify these farms: A) a family plot of cassava and plantain near Mbalmayo; B) a large banana estate with a packing station; C) a farmer who grows cocoa to sell to a buyer.", ["A produces mainly for the family: subsistence.", "B is a large estate with many workers and processing: plantation.", "C grows a cash crop for sale: commercial farming."], "A subsistence, B plantation, C commercial."),
     ("Example 2: reasoning", "Why is cotton grown in the north of Cameroon and not in the equatorial forest?", ["Cotton needs a wet season for growth and a dry season for harvest.", "The north has a savanna climate with a dry season; the south has rain nearly all year.", "Rain at harvest spoils the cotton, so the dry north suits it."], "Because the savanna climate gives a dry season for ripening and harvesting.")],
    (fig, "Types of farming", "Three boxes labelled subsistence, commercial and plantation farming with example crops."),
    [MCQ("Which crop is mainly a cash crop?", "Cocoa", ["Cassava eaten at home", "Plantain for the family", "A vegetable garden"], "Cocoa is grown mainly for sale and export."),
     TF("Shifting cultivation involves moving to a new plot after some years.", True, "The old plot is left to recover."),
     MATCH("Match each crop to its usual region.", [("Cotton", "Savanna of the North"), ("Tea", "Highland slopes"), ("Cocoa", "Forest zone")], "Each crop depends on climate and soil.", tier="application"),
     MCQ("Which is a problem for farmers in Cameroon?", "Poor roads to market", ["Too many tractors", "Constant sunshine", "Very low population"], "Crops spoil before reaching markets.", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Give three ways of improving farming in Cameroon.", "Improve roads and storage; give farmers credit, training and better seeds and fertilisers; form cooperatives and process crops locally; irrigate dry areas.", "1 mark for each of three realistic measures with brief explanation, 3 marks total.", points=3, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("A farmer near Bamenda grows maize and beans for the family and sells the surplus in the market. He also keeps a few cattle.",
          [("mcq", "His farm is best described as", "mixed subsistence farming", ["plantation farming", "fishing", "forestry"], "He grows food mainly for the family and keeps animals.", {}),
           ("tf", "A surplus is the part of the harvest not needed by the family.", True, "It can be sold.", {}),
           ("mcq", "Which of the following would increase his harvest?", "Using improved seeds and fertiliser", ["Clearing less land", "Burning all his crops", "Stopping weeding"], "Inputs and better methods raise yields.", {}),
           ("open", "Explain one difficulty he may face in selling his produce.", "Poor roads and few vehicles make transport slow and costly, so produce can spoil and he receives low prices.", "1 mark for the difficulty; 1 mark for the reason.", {})])],
    [("Plantain and cassava in a family field are examples of", "Subsistence crops", ["Plantation crops only", "Industrial products", "Forest products"], "They feed the family."),
     ("A large estate with many workers is called a", "Plantation", ["Subsistence plot", "Market garden", "Fallow"], "Plantations are large commercial farms."),
     ("Which area is known for cattle rearing?", "Adamawa plateau", ["Coastal swamps", "Mangrove forests", "Mount Cameroon summit"], "The grasslands suit cattle."),
     ("Crop rotation helps to", "Keep the soil fertile", ["Dry the soil", "Increase pests", "Reduce water"], "Different crops use and return different nutrients."),
     ("Cooperatives help farmers by", "Selling and buying in groups", ["Closing markets", "Cutting trees", "Lowering yields"], "Together they get better prices and inputs.")],
    ["Crop-to-region links are given in general terms; a teacher should check them (esp. tea, rubber, Arabica/Robusta zones) against local sources."])

