L = lesson(c4, "transport-trade-tourism", "Transport, trade and tourism",
    ["Compare road, rail, air and water transport", "Explain why transport is important", "Define imports, exports and the balance of trade", "Describe tourism, its benefits and its problems in Cameroon"],
    [("text", "**Transport** moves people and goods. **Roads** are the most widely used way in Cameroon and reach villages, but many are poor in the rainy season. **Railways** carry heavy goods and passengers over long distances: the main line runs from Douala through Yaoundé to Ngaoundéré. **Air transport** is fast and expensive. **Water transport** (ports at Douala, Kribi and Limbe; river transport on the Benue in the rainy season) is cheap for heavy goods."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Why transport matters", "Good transport links bring markets to farmers, goods to shops, raw materials to factories and workers to jobs. They help national unity, attract industry and tourists, and allow help to reach people in emergencies. Poor transport raises prices and makes crops rot."),
     ("text", "**Trade** is the buying and selling of goods and services. **Exports** are goods sold to other countries (for Cameroon: crude oil, cocoa, timber, banana, coffee, cotton, aluminium). **Imports** are goods bought from other countries (machinery, vehicles, fuel products, rice, wheat, medicines). The **balance of trade** is the value of exports minus the value of imports: a surplus if positive, a deficit if negative."),
     ("key", "definition", "Regional trade", "Cameroon belongs to the Economic and Monetary Community of Central Africa (CEMAC), whose members share a common currency, the Central African CFA franc. Cameroon also trades with neighbours such as Nigeria and Chad. Regional groups aim to make trade easier by reducing barriers."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Tourism", "Tourism brings income, jobs and foreign money, and encourages conservation. Attractions: Mount Cameroon, the beaches of Limbe and Kribi, Lobé falls, Waza National Park, the Dja and Korup reserves, Bafut and other palaces and festivals. Problems: poor roads and hotels, insecurity in some areas, pollution and damage to nature if it is not managed well."),
     ("key", "attention", "Be careful", "- Exports minus imports gives the balance of trade; do not reverse the order.\n- A trade deficit is not the same as a loss for every business.\n- Keep units and currency in answers (FCFA, billion FCFA).")],
    [("Example 1: balance of trade", "In a year a country exports goods worth 800 billion FCFA and imports goods worth 950 billion FCFA. Calculate the balance of trade.", ["Balance = exports − imports.", "800 − 950 = −150 billion FCFA.", "A negative result is a trade deficit."], "−150 billion FCFA: a deficit."),
     ("Example 2: choose transport", "A trader must send 30 tonnes of cocoa from Yaoundé to the port of Douala. Which mode is best and why?", ["The goods are heavy and not urgent.", "Rail and road both link Yaoundé and Douala, and rail is cheaper for heavy bulk goods.", "Air is far too costly for such a load."], "Rail (or road if rail is unavailable), because it is cheaper for heavy goods.")],
    (fig, "The railway line from Douala to Ngaoundéré (schematic)", "A line with four dots for Douala, Yaoundé, Belabo and Ngaoundéré."),
    [NUM("A country exports goods worth 500 and imports goods worth 420 (billion FCFA). What is the balance of trade?", 80, "500 − 420 = 80, a surplus.", unit="billion FCFA"),
     MCQ("Which transport is usually cheapest for very heavy goods over a long distance?", "Water or rail", ["Air", "Bicycle", "Motorbike"], "Bulk goods move cheaply by water or rail."),
     TF("Exports are goods bought from other countries.", False, "Exports are sold to other countries; imports are bought.", tier="application"),
     MCQ("Which is an important export of Cameroon?", "Cocoa", ["Snow", "Imported rice", "Foreign cars"], "Cocoa is a leading agricultural export.", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Explain two benefits and one problem of tourism for a region such as Limbe.", "Benefits: jobs in hotels and guiding and income from visitors; support for conservation and local crafts. Problem: litter, pollution or pressure on beaches and water if tourism is not managed.", "1 mark for each benefit (2) and 1 for the problem, with brief explanation.", points=3, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("A farmer in a remote village cannot sell his tomatoes because the road floods every rainy season.",
          [("mcq", "The main problem is", "poor transport", ["too much sun", "lack of tomatoes", "high wages"], "The road prevents sale.", {}),
           ("tf", "A better road would probably raise his income.", True, "It would give access to markets.", {}),
           ("mcq", "A way to reduce spoilage of the tomatoes would be", "Drying or processing them", ["Leaving them in the sun", "Burning them", "Planting less"], "Processing adds value and reduces waste.", {}),
           ("open", "State two other benefits of improving the road.", "Easier access to schools, hospitals and markets, cheaper goods, new businesses and faster emergency help.", "1 mark each for two benefits.", {})])],
    [("Imports are goods", "Brought into a country", ["Sold abroad", "Made at home only", "Always free"], "Imports come from other countries."),
     ("Which port is the main port of Cameroon?", "Douala", ["Garoua", "Bamenda", "Maroua"], "Douala is the main seaport."),
     ("CEMAC's common currency is", "The Central African CFA franc", ["The naira", "The euro", "The dollar"], "CEMAC members use the same currency."),
     ("A trade deficit means", "Imports exceed exports", ["Exports exceed imports", "Trade stops", "No trade"], "Balance of trade is negative."),
     ("A major tourist attraction in Cameroon is", "Waza National Park", ["The Sahara glacier", "The Alps", "The Nile delta"], "It is a famous wildlife park.")],
    ["Teacher to verify: the railway route, Kribi port, export/import lists (qualitative only), CEMAC and CFA franc description, and the list of attractions. The FCFA values in worked examples are invented for practice."])

